1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Shake hex-one-ene with orange bromine water, and the

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,920
colour disappears almost at once, even in the

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:06,800
dark.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,120
That's the double bond adding bromine, from the

5
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:10,320
last video.

6
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:13,440
Now add bromine to hexane, an alkane.

7
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:15,920
In the dark, the orange stays.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Shine UV light on it, though, and the

9
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:21,600
colour slowly fades, and fumes of hydrogen bromide

10
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:22,640
come off.

11
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:25,840
So alkanes can react with bromine after all.

12
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:27,680
Why do they need the light, and where

13
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,680
does that hydrogen bromide come from?

14
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,100
Here's the difference.

15
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:34,980
As you saw last video, an alkene has

16
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:37,460
a weaker second bond it can open, so

17
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:38,460
it adds.

18
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:41,860
An alkane has only single bonds, and every

19
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:43,860
carbon already has four.

20
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:46,500
There's no spare bond for bromine to join,

21
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:48,420
and its C H and C C bonds

22
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:49,540
are strong.

23
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:52,580
So in the dark, at room temperature, nothing

24
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:54,260
you can see happens.

25
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,660
For an alkane to react, a bond has

26
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,620
to break first, and that takes energy.

27
00:01:00,834 --> 00:01:03,113
That's what the UV light supplies.

28
00:01:03,314 --> 00:01:05,873
It gives the energy to split bromine molecules

29
00:01:05,954 --> 00:01:08,753
apart, and that starts the reaction.

30
00:01:08,834 --> 00:01:11,873
The overall result is a swap: one hydrogen

31
00:01:11,914 --> 00:01:14,753
on the alkane is replaced by a bromine.

32
00:01:14,834 --> 00:01:18,193
Ethane plus bromine gives bromoethane.

33
00:01:18,234 --> 00:01:20,193
The hydrogen that's pushed out has to go

34
00:01:20,274 --> 00:01:23,073
somewhere, so it joins the other bromine atom

35
00:01:23,154 --> 00:01:26,193
as hydrogen bromide, the fumes in the hook.

36
00:01:26,234 --> 00:01:29,233
Two products, and every carbon keeps four single

37
00:01:29,274 --> 00:01:30,193
bonds.

38
00:01:30,394 --> 00:01:32,433
This is a substitution reaction.

39
00:01:33,634 --> 00:01:35,233
So what goes on the arrow?

40
00:01:35,714 --> 00:01:38,793
Write UV light, and don't call it a

41
00:01:38,834 --> 00:01:39,553
catalyst.

42
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:42,673
From Module five, a catalyst is a substance

43
00:01:42,754 --> 00:01:44,753
that speeds up a reaction and comes out

44
00:01:44,834 --> 00:01:45,713
unchanged.

45
00:01:46,274 --> 00:01:48,353
UV light isn't a substance at all.

46
00:01:48,674 --> 00:01:51,313
It's energy, and it's absorbed as the reaction

47
00:01:51,394 --> 00:01:53,873
starts, so it's the energy source.

48
00:01:54,514 --> 00:01:56,993
Compare an alkene, which adds bromine at room

49
00:01:57,074 --> 00:01:58,993
temperature with no light at all.

50
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:02,040
Put the two side by side.

51
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:07,560
Ethene plus bromine makes one product, one, two-dibromoethane,

52
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:08,840
and nothing is lost.

53
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:13,400
Ethane plus bromine makes two: bromoethane and hydrogen

54
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:14,320
bromide.

55
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:17,080
So an H X second product is the

56
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:18,520
sign of substitution.

57
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,440
In the models, ethene's flat carbons turn tetrahedral

58
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:23,960
as the double bond opens.

59
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:27,400
Ethane's carbons were tetrahedral already, and they stay

60
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:27,960
that way.

61
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:30,600
One atom just takes another's place.

62
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,840
Substitution is also harder to control.

63
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:37,800
With more chlorine, methane can swap a second

64
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:40,920
hydrogen, then a third and a fourth, all

65
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:44,520
the way to tetrachloromethane, and each swap makes

66
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,360
one more H Cl.

67
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:48,440
And in a longer alkane, the swap can

68
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,440
happen at different carbons.

69
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:54,840
Propane can give one-chloropropane, or two-chloropropane.

70
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,600
So substitution usually gives a mixture, and questions

71
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:00,600
often ask you to write the equation for

72
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:02,040
just one product.

73
00:03:03,267 --> 00:03:04,746
Now alkynes.

74
00:03:04,947 --> 00:03:07,186
A triple bond has two of those weaker

75
00:03:07,347 --> 00:03:10,466
extra bonds, so an alkyne can add twice.

76
00:03:10,787 --> 00:03:16,546
Ethyne plus one bromine molecule gives one, two-dibromoethene.

77
00:03:16,627 --> 00:03:18,386
It still has a double bond, so its

78
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:21,106
name still ends in ene, and it can

79
00:03:21,187 --> 00:03:22,226
add again.

80
00:03:22,307 --> 00:03:25,026
With a second bromine molecule, that bond opens

81
00:03:25,147 --> 00:03:29,826
too, giving one, one, two, two-tetrabromoethane.

82
00:03:29,947 --> 00:03:32,866
Each step puts one piece on each carbon.

83
00:03:32,987 --> 00:03:36,146
Watch the model too: straight-line ethyne goes flat

84
00:03:36,227 --> 00:03:39,826
after one step, then tetrahedral after two, once

85
00:03:39,907 --> 00:03:42,626
each carbon has only single bonds.

86
00:03:42,667 --> 00:03:45,346
An alkene has only one extra bond, so

87
00:03:45,507 --> 00:03:46,866
it adds just once.

88
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:50,866
So with an alkyne, always check how much

89
00:03:50,947 --> 00:03:51,986
reagent there is.

90
00:03:52,467 --> 00:03:55,586
With hydrogen and a nickel catalyst, ethyne becomes

91
00:03:55,747 --> 00:03:57,906
ethene, then ethane.

92
00:03:58,147 --> 00:04:00,706
With just one H two for each ethyne,

93
00:04:00,747 --> 00:04:02,106
you'd write the first step.

94
00:04:02,307 --> 00:04:04,946
If a question says excess, meaning more than

95
00:04:05,027 --> 00:04:08,306
enough, go all the way: ethyne plus two

96
00:04:08,467 --> 00:04:10,306
H two gives ethane.

97
00:04:10,547 --> 00:04:12,746
Hydrogen bromide works the same way.

98
00:04:12,947 --> 00:04:15,826
The first step gives bromoethene, and a second

99
00:04:15,907 --> 00:04:18,066
H Br adds in the same way, giving

100
00:04:18,107 --> 00:04:19,666
a dibromoethane.

101
00:04:20,867 --> 00:04:23,586
Water adds to alkynes too, but the first

102
00:04:23,667 --> 00:04:26,546
product rearranges into a different family, which you'll

103
00:04:26,627 --> 00:04:27,186
meet later.

104
00:04:27,667 --> 00:04:30,146
So water questions in this course are usually

105
00:04:30,307 --> 00:04:31,506
about alkenes.

106
00:04:31,987 --> 00:04:34,546
And now the bromine water test makes sense.

107
00:04:35,027 --> 00:04:38,066
Alkenes and alkynes both decolourise it at room

108
00:04:38,147 --> 00:04:41,666
temperature, without UV light, because both can add.

109
00:04:42,067 --> 00:04:43,346
An alkane doesn't.

110
00:04:43,907 --> 00:04:45,826
So losing the colour shows a double or

111
00:04:45,907 --> 00:04:47,746
triple bond, but not which.

112
00:04:48,307 --> 00:04:50,866
To tell them apart, use the molecular formula.

113
00:04:51,347 --> 00:04:54,786
For an open-chain hydrocarbon with one multiple bond,

114
00:04:54,867 --> 00:04:57,426
C n H two n is an alkene,

115
00:04:57,507 --> 00:04:59,506
and C n H two n minus two

116
00:04:59,747 --> 00:05:00,706
is an alkyne.

117
00:05:01,934 --> 00:05:03,773
So here's the answer to the question.

118
00:05:04,014 --> 00:05:08,573
With halogens, alkanes swap: UV light, one H

119
00:05:08,654 --> 00:05:11,453
out, one halogen in, and H X as

120
00:05:11,534 --> 00:05:12,813
a second product.

121
00:05:13,134 --> 00:05:17,213
Alkenes add once, and alkynes add twice, one

122
00:05:17,294 --> 00:05:20,173
piece on each carbon, with nothing lost.

123
00:05:20,294 --> 00:05:24,413
Alkanes swap, alkenes add, alkynes add twice.

124
00:05:25,634 --> 00:05:26,273
Your turn.

125
00:05:26,834 --> 00:05:30,473
Propane reacts with chlorine gas under UV light.

126
00:05:30,754 --> 00:05:33,393
Identify the type of reaction, and write a

127
00:05:33,594 --> 00:05:36,273
balanced equation for the formation of one organic

128
00:05:36,354 --> 00:05:38,873
product, using structural formulas.

129
00:05:39,154 --> 00:05:40,433
Pause here to answer.

130
00:05:50,834 --> 00:05:54,193
Propane is an alkane, and there's UV light,

131
00:05:54,314 --> 00:05:55,793
so this is substitution.

132
00:05:56,274 --> 00:05:58,353
One hydrogen swaps for a chlorine.

133
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:01,313
C H three, C H two, C H

134
00:06:01,394 --> 00:06:04,993
three, plus Cl two, gives C H three,

135
00:06:05,074 --> 00:06:09,153
C H two, C H two Cl, one-chloropropane,

136
00:06:09,234 --> 00:06:10,753
plus H Cl.

137
00:06:11,154 --> 00:06:13,873
Two-chloropropane is just as correct.

138
00:06:13,914 --> 00:06:15,953
Leaving out the H Cl is the classic

139
00:06:16,114 --> 00:06:16,833
slip.

140
00:06:16,874 --> 00:06:19,153
The swapped hydrogen has to go somewhere.

141
00:06:20,367 --> 00:06:21,006
Last one.

142
00:06:21,087 --> 00:06:24,926
Propyne reacts with excess bromine at room temperature.

143
00:06:25,487 --> 00:06:28,606
Explain why each propyne molecule reacts with two

144
00:06:28,687 --> 00:06:32,326
bromine molecules, and name the final organic product.

145
00:06:32,607 --> 00:06:33,966
Pause here to answer.

146
00:06:44,367 --> 00:06:47,246
Propyne has a triple bond, with two weaker

147
00:06:47,327 --> 00:06:48,406
extra bonds.

148
00:06:48,687 --> 00:06:52,446
The first bromine opens one, giving a dibromopropene,

149
00:06:52,527 --> 00:06:53,886
which still has a double bond.

150
00:06:54,527 --> 00:06:56,846
The bromine is in excess, so the second

151
00:06:56,927 --> 00:06:59,966
bond opens too, putting two bromines on each

152
00:07:00,007 --> 00:07:05,086
of those carbons: one, one, two, two-tetrabromopropane.

153
00:07:05,807 --> 00:07:09,246
Stopping at the dibromopropene is the classic mistake.

154
00:07:09,727 --> 00:07:12,046
Excess means go all the way.

155
00:07:13,267 --> 00:07:15,266
So the bonds decide the reaction.

156
00:07:15,747 --> 00:07:19,186
Alkanes swap, but only with UV light, while

157
00:07:19,267 --> 00:07:22,706
alkenes and alkynes add, and lose bromine water's

158
00:07:22,787 --> 00:07:24,186
colour as they do.

159
00:07:24,627 --> 00:07:28,866
Next, the practical: testing hydrocarbons with bromine water,

160
00:07:28,907 --> 00:07:30,386
safely, in the lab.

161
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:33,040
Here are the key notes.

162
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:35,200
Pause the video if you need more time

163
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:36,080
to copy them.
